<!--
  Managed by infra-hetzner/ansible/roles/app — do not edit by hand.

  Credentials file consumed by `clickhouse-client` (read via its
  config-file option) from the host-side backup script. Mounted
  read-only into the CH container at /etc/clickhouse-backup/client.xml
  via the production compose overlay; the client picks up user
  and password from this file instead of from argv or environment.

  ## Why no double-hyphen anywhere in this comment

  XML 1.0 §2.5 forbids the literal sequence "‑‑" inside a comment
  body (it terminates the comment). Poco XML (the parser CH uses)
  is strict about this and rejects the file outright with
  "Invalid token". Spell CLI flags as words rather than copying
  the literal `‑‑flag` form into the comment.

  ## Mode 0400 owner=101 — group intentionally absent

  GID 101 on Ubuntu 24.04 is allocated dynamically by `useradd`
  and may map to an unrelated system group (`messagebus`,
  `systemd-resolve`, …) which would inadvertently gain read on
  the plaintext password. With mode 0400 and a numeric owner
  matching the in-container CH UID, only the CH container user
  can read the file; no host group is involved.

  ## Password XML-escape via CDATA

  Jinja2 autoescape is off for `.xml.j2` (autoescape triggers
  only on `.html`/`.htm` by default), so a password containing
  `&`, `<`, `>`, `'`, or `"` would otherwise produce invalid XML
  and break CH config parsing on startup. CDATA neutralises the
  special-char interpretation; the only sequence that breaks
  CDATA is `]]>`, which is rejected by the env-source CN/password
  validation upstream.
-->
<config>
  <user>default</user>
  <password><![CDATA[{{ clickhouse_password }}]]></password>
  <host>127.0.0.1</host>
  <port>9000</port>
</config>
